---
<BaseLayout
  title="Hassam Shabbir: Psychologist, Writer and Author Bio"
  description="Hassam Shabbir is the psychologist, writer and author behind Sports Performance Psychology. Read his first-person profile, editorial role, and credentials."
>
  <article>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Author</p>
      <p class="kicker">Professional profile</p>
      <div class="author-head">
        <img
          src="/images/hassam-shabbir-author.webp"
          alt="Portrait of Hassam Shabbir"
          width="320"
          height="320"
          loading="eager"
        />
        <div>
          <h1>Hassam Shabbir | Psychologist, Writer and Author</h1>
          <p>I am the psychologist, writer, and author behind Sports Performance Psychology. Every article on this site is written by me, in my own words, and checked against the sources I cite.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose">

        <h2>Psychology training shaped by the responsibility to explain</h2>
        <p>My formal psychology education began in Pakistan with a B.S. in Psychology from the University of Sargodha, and continued through professional study from institutions in the United States and Canada. Along the way I studied sport and performance psychology in depth: its degree routes, its credentials, its research literature, and how its findings actually reach athletes.</p>
        <p>My professional work is remote and international, including projects and clients in the United States. It centers on research, writing, content strategy, and psychology-informed communication for complex health and education topics. My broader writing portfolio includes more than 1,000 articles across healthcare, education, technology, and other fields.</p>
        <p>As founder of Rise Above Minds, I lead a nonprofit initiative that shares mental health and psychology education with children and communities. I have also trained more than 400 professionals in AI and prompt engineering. Today I work as a Senior SEO/AEO/GEO Specialist at Quality Resource, where I lead search initiatives across multiple brands, which taught me something this site is built on: the best information in the world is useless if the people who need it cannot find it or understand it.</p>

        <h2>A clear claim with a clear boundary</h2>
        <p>This profile documents my editorial role, completed learning, and selected work. Course certificates listed below are presented as continued study, not as a degree, professional license, or specialty certification.</p>
        <p>What I do: I write educational articles about mental performance, I research degrees and career routes so readers can compare them honestly, and I review evidence and sources for this publication before anything is published.</p>
        <p>What I do not do through this publication: I do not diagnose conditions, I do not give treatment advice, and I do not provide clinical care. Nothing on this site creates a psychologist-patient relationship, and I will not pretend that educational writing can substitute for one.</p>

        <h2>The subjects behind the byline</h2>
        <p>My writing on this publication stays inside four areas of focus. These describe the educational topics I research and explain; they do not replace case-specific assessment or professional advice.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Sport and performance psychology education: what the field studies and how its findings apply to performance.</li>
          <li>Degrees and certification routes: honest, sourced guides to programs, credentials, and costs.</li>
          <li>Evidence communication: figures and claims presented with their sources attached, never without.</li>
          <li>Mental skills training: focus, confidence, routines, and visualization for healthy athletes and performers.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Writing, evidence review and correction</h2>
        <p>My role on this publication follows a fixed process for every article, and it is described in full in our <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a>:</p>
        <ol class="process-steps">
          <li>Define the reader's real question, not the keyword, before a single word is written.</li>
          <li>Verify every factual claim against reliable sources, and cite them where they can be checked.</li>
          <li>Check the limits and context of each finding: who it studied, what it cannot prove, and where it does not apply.</li>
          <li>Edit for accuracy in plain language, cutting anything that cannot be supported.</li>
          <li>Review published work on a rolling basis and correct errors openly when I find them or a reader reports one.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Selected completed learning</h2>
        <p>Course titles and issuing institutions are shown for context. Listing a course does not imply a degree, institutional endorsement, or a separate professional license. These are the parts of my continued study most relevant to sport and performance psychology; my complete course list appears on my <a href="https://forensicpsychology.pro/author/hassam-shabbir/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">forensic psychology author profile</a>.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>B.S. in Psychology, University of Sargodha</li>
          <li>University of Pennsylvania: Positive Psychology, Martin E. P. Seligman's Visionary Science</li>
          <li>Wesleyan University: Creating Behavioral Change</li>
          <li>University of Toronto: The Arts and Science of Relationships, Understanding Human Needs</li>
          <li>Johns Hopkins University: Psychological First Aid</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Selected independent work</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Author of <em>Soch Say Sajjday Tak</em>, an Urdu book bringing psychology and Islamic teachings into conversation around mental noise, rumination, mindfulness, attention, and spiritual wellbeing, themes at the heart of mental-skills training.</li>
          <li>Founder of Rise Above Minds, a nonprofit sharing mental health and psychology education with children and communities.</li>
          <li>Writing portfolio of more than 1,000 articles across healthcare, education, technology, and other fields.</li>
          <li>Trained more than 400 professionals in AI and prompt engineering.</li>
          <li>Author of SEO and GEO eBooks and a Prompt Playbook.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Articles by Hassam Shabbir</h2>
        <p>Every guide links to its sources, publication date, evidence-review date, and editorial standards. Articles are listed from newest to oldest.</p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>
            <a href="/blog/doctorate-in-sports-psychology/">Doctorate in Sports Psychology: PhD Programs Guide (2026)</a>
            <span class="article-date">November 12, 2026</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/blog/bachelors-in-sports-psychology/">Bachelor's in Sports Psychology: Complete Degree Guide</a>
            <span class="article-date">November 4, 2026</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/blog/visualization-for-athletes/">Visualization for Athletes: A Practical Guide</a>
            <span class="article-date">November 2, 2026</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/blog/sports-psychology-salary/">Sports Psychology Salary: Pay by Role & State</a>
            <span class="article-date">November 1, 2026</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/blog/online-sports-psychology-degree/">Online Sports Psychology Degree: Accredited Options</a>
            <span class="article-date">October 31, 2026</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/blog/sports-psychology-masters-programs/">Sports Psychology Master's Programs: 2026 Guide</a>
            <span class="article-date">October 30, 2026</span>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Follow the evidence behind every article</h2>
        <p>I welcome corrections and source updates. If you spot an error, an outdated figure, or a claim that needs a better source, tell me through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and I will review it. Accuracy is a process, and readers are part of it.</p>

        <h2>Connect with me</h2>
        <ul class="author-social">
          <li><a href="https://www.instagram.com/hassam_shabbir_official/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Instagram</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/hassam-shabbir-sxo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.threads.net/@hassam_shabbir_official" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Threads</a></li>
        </ul>

        <div class="boundary-box" role="note">
          <h2>Educational writing is not individual professional advice</h2>
          <p>Everything on this site is general education about mental performance. It does not create a psychologist-patient relationship, it does not diagnose conditions, and it does not give treatment advice. If you have a question about your own situation, please speak with a qualified professional who can assess it directly.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)}></script>
  </article>
  <style>
    .kicker {
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 800;
      color: var(--red);
      margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .author-head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 26px; margin-top: 18px; }
    .author-head img {
      width: 160px; height: 160px; border-radius: 50%;
      object-fit: cover; flex-shrink: 0;
      border: 3px solid var(--red);
    }
    .author-head h1 { margin: 0 0 10px; font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 38px); }
    .author-head p { margin: 0; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6; }
    .prose h2 { margin-top: 44px; }
    .prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
    .process-steps { padding-left: 24px; }
    .process-steps li { margin-bottom: 10px; line-height: 1.65; }
    .article-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
    .article-list li { margin-bottom: 12px; }
    .article-list a { font-weight: 700; font-size: 17px; }
    .article-date { display: block; font-size: 14px; color: #555; margin-top: 2px; }
    .author-social { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: flex; gap: 22px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .author-social a { font-weight: 700; }
    .boundary-box {
      margin-top: 48px;
      background: #f6f4f1;
      border: 1px solid #d9d2c6;
      border-left: 5px solid var(--red);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 26px 28px;
    }
    .boundary-box h2 { margin: 0 0 10px !important; font-size: 20px; }
    .boundary-box p { margin: 0; line-height: 1.7; }
    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .author-head { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; }
      .author-head img { width: 128px; height: 128px; }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
